  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry through Friday. Cold front approaches the region Friday \nnight returning rain chances to the area for the weekend.\n\nCold air advection stratocu bringing mostly cloud skies to the\nmountains/foothills. Evening RNK sounding shows shallow but \nnear saturated layer of moisture near 800 mb... and forecast\nsoundings maintain this layer through the overnight. Should see\nskies clear once we lose the northerly cold air advection on\nTuesday winds also diminishing for Tuesday.\n\nFor Tuesday, the Nor'easter and its associated upper level \ntrough that have been off the coast of New Jersey and Long \nIsland will finally lift north and east of the region, which \nwill allow for 500mb heights and upper level ridging to build \nback into the region. This will return daytime highs back into \nthe upper 70s to low 80s for mountain locations, and upper 80s \nto low 90s for Piedmont locations starting Wednesday and \ncontinuing through the end of the work week. While temperatures \nwill surge to above normal values again, dewpoints look to \nremain in the 40s/50s which will make the airmass feel much more\ntolerable. \n\nBy Friday night, a broad upper level shortwave trough looks to\nslide east through the northern Great Lakes region. This will\nusher in a cold front passage late on Friday and into Saturday\nmorning, which looks to moderate temperatures back to near \nnormal to start the weekend. This frontal boundary will also\nreturn minimal rain chances to the area. Possible moisture from\na tropical system in the eastern Pacific will eventually track \ninto the southeast by early next week; however, timing and \nspecific details of how much rainfall are still uncertain at \nthis time. This moisture may bring the next best widespread \nchance for rainfall across the region.",
